refactor!: one tool that runs code, instead of 28 tools that each do one thing

The server advertised a catalog of 28 typed verbs, then grew a declarative
composition layer on top of it (pick/save/constrain/output/then). Both are the
same mistake at different sizes: a fixed menu is the wrong interface for a
knowledge base whose callers and targets all differ, and every new capability
needed new hand-wired glue.

Now there is one tool. It runs code in a namespace that persists.

    run(code = "", session_id = "", strings = null) -> str

    > run("await search_skills({ query: 'EPT hook' })")
    > run("const brief = async (n) => (await read_skill({name:n})).slice(0,2000);")
    > run("await brief('ags-kdmapper')")        # still defined, one call later

WHAT REPLACED WHAT

  surface + pick/save/constrain/output/then  ->  one code tool
  28 registered verbs                        ->  27 capabilities, reached from code
  per-model profiles (match.clientNames)     ->  nothing: adaptation is emergent

The capabilities are all still here and all still work. They are captured by a
thin shim over the tool decorator, which validates arguments against each
capability's own schema, and seeded into the namespace as async functions plus a
grouped `kb` namespace. Adding one is still one function.

HOW A CALL RUNS (src/harness.ts)

Code is parsed once with acorn, then split:

  definitions   function/class/var declarations. Run as TOP-LEVEL scripts, so the
                binding outlives the call, and written to disk afterwards.
  work          everything else. Run inside an async wrapper, so `await` works,
                and deliberately NOT persisted: a restart rebuilds the model's
                tools without replaying its work.

The split is forced by two things pulling in opposite directions — a top-level
script cannot await, and an async function's bindings die with the call. Each
kind runs where it belongs and both work.

Persistence assigns through `globalThis` rather than declaring, because a `var`
or function binding is NON-CONFIGURABLE and `forget` could never remove it. One
file per bound name in $GAMEHACKING_HARNESS_DIR, each loaded at boot in its own
try/except: a broken saved definition cannot stop the server booting, and it is
reported on stderr rather than swallowed.

SECURITY: a deployment fact, not a toggle. Running code IS the tool, so there is
no enable/disable flag and no code-free mode — a flag would reframe the design as
an optional feature. Said plainly in the tool description, the server
instructions, and the README: run the server sandboxed, as you would a shell.

BUGS FOUND WHILE BUILDING IT, each now a gate

- the trailing expression was re-run in a fresh wrapper, where call-local
  bindings do not exist. It now comes from the execution that actually happened.
- acorn's ExpressionStatement.end INCLUDES the semicolon, so `return (expr;)` was
  a syntax error and definitions silently never saved.
- `forget` in the same call was undone by the save loop running after work.
- `load()` was written but never called, so restart persistence silently did
  nothing. It now runs before the server accepts a request.
- `describe` reported `any` for every argument: zod v4 keeps the type in
  `_def.type`, and the code read `_def.typeName`.
- my console.log returned `push()`'s value, so a trailing `console.log(...)`
  reported a number where the real console reports undefined.
- the gate harness awaited the `notifications/initialized` promise, which has no
  reply, deadlocking every gate. The battery went from "never finishes" to 11s.

DELETED

  src/profiles/{types,resolve,registry,shipped}.ts   1269 lines
  profiles/{default,manifest}.json
  test/model-profiles.test.ts                        2261 lines
  the surface tool + 9 render/pick/save functions

TESTS AND GATES

  tests  40 (was 97) — the deleted 57 tested the deleted layer
  gates  13, every one verified by restoring its own defect

Two gates had INEFFECTIVE injections at first, and both are recorded rather than
quietly fixed: a declaration's `await` check is not what keeps definitions
persistent (globalThis assignment survives a wrapper), and dropping the index's
single-flight guard no longer reproduces its bug because the chunked build fixes
it independently — so G13's note says what it does NOT prove.

The one-tool shape also removes the mount limitation for free: there is nothing
to mount but `run`, so a host that reads the tool list once sees the whole
surface.
